First, let me list out the components again: Bit, Transmission, Motor, Electrical System, Battery Holder, Housing, External Environment.

Starting with the Bit. The bit is used to drive screws, so it interacts directly with the Transmission because the transmission transfers power from the motor to the bit. So Bit and Transmission are in contact (1). Also, the Bit is part of the Housing since it's attached or stored there when not in use. So Bit interacts with Housing (1). The External Environment would be where the bit is used on materials, so Bit interacts with that environment (1).

Next, Transmission. It connects to Motor because the motor drives the transmission (1). Transmission also connects to Electrical System since it's controlled by electrical signals or power from the system (1). Additionally, Transmission interacts with Housing as part of the tool's structure (1).

Motor: The Motor is connected to Electrical System because it needs electricity to function (1). It also interacts with Battery Holder if it's a cordless screwdriver powered by a battery (1). The Motor is inside the Housing, so they interact (1).

Electrical System: This system connects to Battery Holder in cordless tools for power supply (1). It also interfaces with Transmission and Motor for control and power distribution (1 each). The Electrical System is part of the Housing as well.

Battery Holder: It holds the battery which powers the Motor, so it interacts with Motor (1) and Electrical System (1). Also, it's part of the Housing structure (1).

Housing: Encases all components, so it interacts with Bit, Transmission, Motor, Electrical System, Battery Holder. The External Environment is outside the housing but interacts through its interface.

External Environment: Interacts with Bit when driving screws into materials. It also interacts with Housing as that's where the tool operates in the environment.

